# baseURI: https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/
#
# starlayer.graph's and starlayer.sparql's own self-declared RDF 1.2/
# SPARQL 1.2 conformance statements (2026-10-09), two real resources -
# not one invented "whole library" node - following the same
# dcterms:conformsTo pattern starshacl's own declaration already uses
# (starlayer/shacl/assets/shacl12-conformance-profile.ttl), which is
# merged into the same combined graph unchanged (its own subject, not
# re-homed here) by starlayer.conformance.declared_conformance().
#
# The dcterms:conformsTo targets themselves are added programmatically by
# that function - not hand-copied into this file - to avoid two
# independently-drifting copies of the same claims. This file itself only
# carries each resource's own identity metadata (label/comment/modified).
#
# Conformance is per docs/graph/rdf12_sparql12_gap_analysis.md's own "no
# open items... a confirmed match" verdict (last reviewed 2026-09-20)
# across all seven tracked documents - re-verify against that document's
# own "Status" section before relying on this at a later date, since RDF
# 1.2 is still Candidate Recommendation stage and can still move.
#
# RDF/XML 1.2 (starlayer.graph) is claimed within documented scope limits
# (only node elements directly under <rdf:RDF> and their direct property
# children are inspected - see that gap-analysis doc's S3 for the exact
# limits), not excluded outright - no RDF-level predicate exists for
# expressing that nuance (see starlayer/conformance.py's own module
# docstring for why), so it's recorded here only as prose.
#
# JSON-LD "1.2"/TriX "1.2" (also starlayer.graph) are not claimed, but not
# because of any implementation gap - neither has a real RDF 1.2
# companion spec to converge on at all (see the gap-analysis doc S6), so
# there is nothing to assert conformance *to* for either one.
#
# SPARQL has no named-profile mechanism analogous to SHACL 1.2 Profiling
# S3 at all - confirmed directly against the SPARQL 1.2 Query spec itself,
# which defines only hierarchical *version* labels ("1.1"/"1.2-basic"/
# "1.2"), not a feature-area breakdown with its own IRIs
# (http://www.w3.org/ns/sparql/profile/ is not a real vocabulary) - so
# starlayer.sparql's own claim below is just dcterms:conformsTo the
# relevant real TR documents directly, with no named sub-profile IRIs to
# choose among.
#
# Three more documents added (2026-10-09), found by auditing the
# project's own docs/code for every W3C document mentioned rather than
# assuming the original seven above were exhaustive - see
# starlayer/conformance.py's own module docstring for the full account:
# RDF Dataset Canonicalization (RDFC-1.0) and SPARQL 1.2 Protocol
# (starlayer.graph), SRL (SPARQL Rules) (starlayer.sparql). Each has its
# own documented, narrow scope limits (see that module docstring), same
# "claim within documented limits, not an exclusion" treatment as RDF/XML.
#
# SPARQL 1.2 Entailment Regimes is deliberately NOT claimed as a
# document - three of its six regimes (full OWL 2 RDF-Based Semantics,
# D-Entailment, RIF Core) are genuinely unimplemented, not just
# scope-limited. Instead, starlayer.graph's resource asserts
# dcterms:conformsTo each *individually* supported regime IRI
# (http://www.w3.org/ns/entailment/{RDF,RDFS,OWL-RDF-Based,OWL-Direct} -
# each confirmed live, dereferenceable) - the same shape as SHACL's own
# named profiles, reused directly from entailment_regimes.SUPPORTED_REGIMES
# rather than claiming the document whole.
#
# D-Entailment specifically also gets an explicit
# conformance:doesNotConformTo <http://www.w3.org/ns/entailment/D> triple
# (added programmatically in starlayer/conformance.py, alongside the
# positive regime claims above, not hand-written here) - it has a real,
# known, dereferenceable IRI and the exclusion is total, so a clean
# negative claim is possible, unlike full OWL 2 RDF-Based Semantics
# (shares the exact same IRI this project already partially claims
# dcterms:conformsTo for) or RIF Core (no IRI defined for it at all) -
# both of those stay prose-only, for lack of any IRI to assert a negative
# claim against. See starlayer/conformance.py's own module docstring for
# the full account of why this new predicate was minted at all, and
# exactly where it is and isn't used.

@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/tree/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph>
    rdfs:label "starlayer.graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "RDF 1.2 graph management - the rdflib replacement, with triple-term and direction-tagged-literal support."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Not claimed: the full SPARQL 1.2 Entailment Regimes document (D-Entailment, full OWL 2 RDF-Based Semantics, and RIF Core Entailment are unimplemented - see the individually-listed supported regimes above instead), nor JSON-LD \"1.2\"/TriX \"1.2\" (neither has a real RDF 1.2 companion spec to converge on at all)."@en ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-09"^^xsd:date .

<https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/tree/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql>
    rdfs:label "starlayer.sparql" ;
    rdfs:comment "SPARQL 1.2 query/update grammar, rewriting, and rule vocabularies (SRL/SQE) over RDF 1.2 graphs."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "SRL has documented grammar scope reductions - no ','-chained object lists, limited property paths, no reified-triple syntax yet, IMPORTS hard-rejected (see starlayer/sparql/CLAUDE.md)."@en ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-09"^^xsd:date .
